---
title: Card
category: Components
description: A content surface with an optional title, top or bottom image, and a side by side layout.
dir: ".lib/pages"
tags: [card, content, container, display, component, svelte, svelte-5, theui-svelte, component-library, ui, tailwindcss]
---

<script lang="ts">
  import DocLayout from "#lib/ui/doc/DocLayout.svelte";
  import DocHead from "#lib/ui/doc/Head.svelte";
  import DocBlock from "#lib/ui/doc/Block.svelte";
  import Code from "#lib/ui/doc/CodeBlock.svelte";
  import Example from "#lib/ui/doc/Example.svelte";
  import ConfigComponent from "#lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import { processID, editURL } from "#lib";
  let { component }: {component: any } = $props();

  import { Card, Button } from "theui-svelte";

  const imgData = {src: "https://placehold.co/800x400/6A00F5/white", alt: "A demo image"}
  const imgDataY = {src: "https://placehold.co/300x600/6A00F5/white", alt: "A demo image"}
</script>

<DocLayout>
  <DocHead title="Card" text="The Card component wraps content in a surface with an optional title, an image above or below it, and a side by side layout." edit_url={editURL("card")} />

  <DocBlock title="About">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Card</code> component groups a title, an image and some body content into one block. The layout, the images and the classes are all set through props. The image can sit above the content, below it, or behind it with a scrim over the top.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Example">
    <p class="not-prose">Start by importing the Card component from the theui-svelte library:</p>
    <Code>

```html
<script>
  import { Card } from "theui-svelte";
</script>
```
    </Code>
    <p class="not-prose">Here is the <code>Card</code> with a title, an image and some body content. The layout and the styling are adjusted with the props listed further down.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96" title="Card Title" topImage={imgData}>
          <p>A short line of body content, so the card has something to wrap around.</p>
          <div>
            <Button>Submit</Button>
          </div>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<script>
  const imgData = {src: "https://placehold.co/800x400/6A00F5/white", alt: "A demo image"}
</script>

<Card title="Card Title" topImage={imgData}>
  <p>A short line of body content, so the card has something to wrap around.</p>
  <div>
    <Button>Submit</Button>
  </div>
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <p class="not-prose">Alternatively you can use related <b>snippet</b> for the card item like title and images.</p>
    <Code>

```html
<Card>
  {#snippet topImage()}
    <img class="block w-full rounded-t-md" src="..." alt="...">
  {/snippet}

  {#snippet title()}
    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold">Card Title</h4>
  {/snippet}

  <p>A short line of body content, so the card has something to wrap around.</p>
  <div>
    <Button>Submit</Button>
  </div>
</Card>
```
    </Code>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Card Elements">
    <p class="not-prose">There are some prefix elements in the <code>Card</code> component. The example are showing below.</p>
    <Example title="Card Title">
      <p class="not-prose">You can use either <code>title prop</code> or <code>title snippet</code> to add card title.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96" title="Card Title">
          ...
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card title="Card Title">
  ...
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">You can use the <code>title</code> snippet also:</p>

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card>
  {#snippet title()}
    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold">Card Title</h4>
  {/snippet}
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <Example title="Card's Top Image">
      <p class="not-prose">Use either the <code>topImage prop</code> or <code>topImage snippet</code> to add image at the top in the <code>Card</code> component.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96" topImage={imgData} title="Card Title">
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<script>
  const imgData = {src: "https://placehold.co/800x400/6A00F5/white", alt: "A demo image"}
</script>

<Card title="Card Title" topImage={imgData}>
  ...
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <Example title="Card's Bottom Image">
      <p class="not-prose">Use either the <code>bottomImage prop</code> or <code>bottomImage snippet</code> to add image at the bottom in the <code>Card</code> component.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96" bottomImage={imgData} title="Card Title">
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<script>
  const imgData = {src: "https://placehold.co/800x400/6A00F5/white", alt: "A demo image"}
</script>

<Card title="Card Title" bottomImage={imgData}>
  ...
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">Or, use <code>bottomImage</code> snippet:</p>
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Card>
  {#snippet title()}
    <h4 class="text-xl font-semibold">Card Title</h4>
  {/snippet}

  {#snippet bottomImage()}
    <img class="block w-full rounded-b-md" src="..." alt="...">
  {/snippet}

  <p>This is card content..</p>
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <Example title="Card Content">
      <p class="not-prose">Any element the <code>Card</code> component will be the card content.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96">
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card>
  <p>This is card content..</p>
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

    <Example title="Image Overlay">
      <p class="not-prose">Use the <code>imageOverlay</code> prop to place the card title and content over the image, with a backdrop behind the text for readability. Set it to <code>true</code> for the default backdrop, or pass a string of classes to style the backdrop. The default value is <code>false</code>.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96" topImage={imgData} title="Card Title" imageOverlay={true}>
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card title="Card Title" topImage={imgData} imageOverlay={true}>
  ...
</Card>

<!-- Custom backdrop classes -->
<Card title="Card Title" topImage={imgData} imageOverlay="bg-black/70">
  ...
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Horizontal Card">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Card</code> component supports a flexible layout option that allows you to switch between vertical and horizontal orientations, depending on your design needs. To adopt a horizontal layout for the <code>Card</code> component, simply add the <code>horizontal</code> attribute. When this attribute is present, the card's content will be arranged side by side, making it ideal for designs where a wide layout is preferred.</p>

    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96" bottomImage={imgDataY} title="Card Title" horizontal>
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card horizontal>
  <p>This is card content..</p>
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Rounded Corner">
    <p class="not-prose">Control the corner of the card using the <code>rounded</code> prop. Available values are: <code>sm</code>, <code>md</code>, <code>lg</code>, <code>xl</code>, <code>2xl</code>, <code>full</code> and <code>none</code>, Default value is <code>md</code></p>

    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <Card class="w-96" topImage={imgData} title="Card Title" rounded="none">
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
        <Card class="w-96" topImage={imgData} title="Card Title" rounded="xl">
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      <div>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card ... rounded="none"> ... </Card>
<Card ... rounded="xl"> ... </Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>

  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Customization">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Card</code> component is easy to customize, letting you style it to match your needs. You can change how any part of the card looks. Whether it's the content, title, shadow, or shape, you have full control.</p>
    <p class="not-prose">You can either use snippets for custom components or utilize these props directly for more straightforward customization. Here are the key props available for customization:</p>
    <ul class="mt-0 mb-0 list-inside">
      <li><b>class</b>: Apply custom styles to the card's container.</li>
      <li><b>contentClasses</b>: Apply custom styles to the card's content.</li>
      <li><b>titleClasses</b>: Style the title with your preferred classes.</li>
      <li><b>shadow</b>: Adjust the card's shadow for a more or less prominent effect.</li>
      <li><b>rounded</b>: Control the card's border-radius to achieve the desired shape.</li>
    </ul>

    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
      <div>
        <Card class="w-96 border-2 border-green-500 bg-green-100" title="Card Title" titleClasses="bg-green-500 p-2 rounded-md text-green-50" contentClasses="text-green-500" shadow="none" rounded="xl">
          <p>This is card content..</p>
        </Card>
      </div>
      {/snippet}

      {#snippet code()}
```html
<Card
  class="border-2 border-green-500 bg-green-100"
  titleClasses="bg-green-500 p-2 rounded-md text-green-50"
  contentClasses="text-green-500"
  shadow="none"
  rounded="xl"
  title="Card Title"
>
  <p>This is card content..</p>
</Card>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
    <p class="not-prose mb-0">Anything else you pass reaches the element itself, so an <code>id</code>, a <code>title</code>, a <code>data-*</code> attribute or an event handler all work the way they would on a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Accessibility">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Card</code> component is a plain container, so screen readers read its content in order without announcing the card itself. A string <code>title</code> is rendered as an <code>&lt;h4&gt;</code> heading, letting users jump to each card with heading navigation. If you use the <code>title</code> snippet, use a heading element there too. Give image props a meaningful <code>alt</code> text (or an empty one for decorative images), and when using <code>imageOverlay</code>, make sure the text keeps enough contrast against the image.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Configuration">
    <ConfigComponent {component} />
  </DocBlock>

